/-!
# Finite graded extensions and projective spectra

Source: Stacks Project, Constructions, Tag 01MY (lemma-morphism-proj), with
Algebra, Tags 07Z2(2), 00GK and 00GQ, and Morphisms, Tag 01WI
(blueprint: lem-per-finite-graded-proj-morphism).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u

open CategoryTheory HomogeneousLocalization

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- An injective module-finite graded ring map induces a finite surjective morphism
on the whole projective spectrum, with the canonical maps on standard affine charts.
No identification of the two degree-zero rings is required.

Source: Stacks Project, Constructions, Tag 01MY (lemma-morphism-proj), and Algebra,
Tags 07Z2(2), 00GK, 00GQ; Morphisms, Tag 01WI
(blueprint: lem-per-finite-graded-proj-morphism).

Proof:
1. Replace finite module generators of A by their homogeneous components and include 1.
   If N bounds their degrees, every homogeneous element of degree greater than N belongs
   to the ideal generated by images of positive-degree elements of C. A homogeneous prime
   of Proj A misses a positive-degree element a, hence also sufficiently high powers of a.
   It therefore misses some image of a positive-degree homogeneous element of C. Thus the
   source standard opens indexed by such elements of C cover all of Proj A.
2. On the f chart use HomogeneousLocalization.Away.map, sending c/f^m to
   iota(c)/iota(f)^m. Localization relations make this well-defined, and injectivity of
   iota makes this chart ring map injective.
3. The standard-open intersection formula (lem-weil-pbc-proj-basic-open-intersection)
   expresses overlaps through g^d/f^e. The chart maps preserve this element, and on the
   overlap both maps send c/(fg)^m to iota(c)/(iota(f)iota(g))^m. They agree.
4. Scheme-morphism gluing (lem-weil-pbc-scheme-morphism-gluing) gives the morphism r on
   the cover of step 1, characterized by precisely these affine chart maps.
5. On each source g chart, nonvanishing of f^e/g^d pulls back to nonvanishing of
   iota(f)^e/iota(g)^d. The intersection formula therefore gives the inverse image
   D_+(iota(f)) of D_+(f), on all of the source.
6. Regard A as the finite graded C-module with action through iota. Apply
   lem-per-finite-graded-module-dehomogenization, using the finite-type hypothesis on C.
   Its degree-zero module fractions identify with Away A (iota(f)), so the chart ring
   maps are finite. The affine-local criterion lem-weil-pbc-finite-local-on-target
   proves that r is finite.
7. The injective finite chart ring maps are integral by
   lem-weil-pbc-finite-algebra-is-integral. Integral injective lying-over
   (lem-weil-pbc-integral-injective-lying-over) makes their spectrum maps surjective.
   Since the target standard opens cover, r is surjective. Nilpotent f gives empty
   charts, and the argument also allows empty projective spectra. -/
theorem Proj.exists_isFinite_surjective_of_finite_graded
    {C A : Type u} [CommRing C] [CommRing A]
    (𝒞 : ℕ → AddSubgroup C) (𝒜 : ℕ → AddSubgroup A)
    [GradedRing 𝒞] [GradedRing 𝒜] (iota : 𝒞 →+*ᵍ 𝒜)
    (_hinj : Function.Injective iota)
    (_hft : (algebraMap (𝒞 0) C).FiniteType) (_hfinite : iota.toRingHom.Finite) :
    ∃ r : Proj 𝒜 ⟶ Proj 𝒞, IsFinite r ∧ Surjective r ∧
      ∀ (d : ℕ) (hd : 0 < d) (f : C) (hf : f ∈ 𝒞 d),
        r ⁻¹ᵁ Proj.basicOpen 𝒞 f = Proj.basicOpen 𝒜 (iota f) ∧
          Proj.awayι 𝒜 (iota f) (iota.2 hf) hd ≫ r =
            Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (Away.map iota f)) ≫ Proj.awayι 𝒞 f hf hd := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
